%I A399632 #14 Sep 10 2026 22:58:45
%S A399632 63,124,242,78124,48828124,1220703124
%N A399632 Numbers k = p*q^2, where p and q are distinct primes, such that k+1 is a prime power with exponent >= 2.
%C A399632 a(3) = 242 = 2*11^2, with a(3)+1 = 243 = 3^5, realizes the exceptional relation 3^5 - 2*11^2 = 1 in Lemma 2.5 of Khosravi and Zarea Moghanjoghi (2007), which they describe as introduced by Crescenzo and modified by Bugeaud. The only other exceptional relation is 239^2 - 2*13^4 = -1; apart from these two exceptions, every solution of P^m - 2*Q^n = +-1 with P, Q prime and m,n > 1 has m = n = 2.
%C A399632 The q = 2 branch is exactly the subsequence x = 5^m - 1 for m in A004061. Indeed, q = 2 gives x = 4*p with p odd, so r^m = x+1 = 4*p+1 is congruent to 5 mod 8. Hence m is odd and r is congruent to 5 mod 8. Then p = (r^m-1)/4 = ((r-1)/4)*(1+r+...+r^(m-1)). Since the second factor is greater than 1 and p is prime, (r-1)/4 = 1, hence r = 5. Thus p = (5^m-1)/4 is prime. Consequently a(2), a(4), a(5), a(6) = 124, 78124, 48828124, 1220703124 are 4 times the first four terms of A086122, corresponding to m = 3, 7, 11, 13, the first four terms of A004061.
%C A399632 This is the increasing enumeration of {x in A054753 : x+1 is in A246547}.
%C A399632 The q = 2 subsequence is b(k) = 5^A004061(k) - 1 = 4*A086122(k), k >= 1.
%H A399632 P. Crescenzo, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0001-8708(75)90083-3">A Diophantine equation which arises in the theory of finite groups</a>, Advances in Mathematics, 17 (1975), 25-29.
%H A399632 B. Khosravi and A. Zarea Moghanjoghi, <a href="https://www.m-hikari.com/ijcms-password2007/25-28-2007/khosraviIJCMS25-28-2007.pdf">Quasirecognition by prime graph of some alternating groups</a>, Int. J. Contemp. Math. Sciences, 2 (2007), no. 28, 1351-1358.
%e A399632 a(1) = 63 = 7*3^2 (p = 7, q = 3), and a(1)+1 = 64 = 2^6.
%e A399632 a(2) = 124 = 31*2^2 (p = 31, q = 2), and a(2)+1 = 125 = 5^3.
%e A399632 a(3) = 242 = 2*11^2 (p = 2, q = 11), and a(3)+1 = 243 = 3^5.
%e A399632 a(6) = 1220703124 = 305175781*2^2 (p = 305175781, q = 2), and a(6)+1 = 1220703125 = 5^13.
%o A399632 (Python)
%o A399632 from sympy import primerange, factorint
%o A399632 from math import isqrt
%o A399632 B = 1220703125
%o A399632 A = []
%o A399632 for r in primerange(2, isqrt(B) + 1):
%o A399632     t = r*r
%o A399632     while t <= B:
%o A399632         n = t - 1
%o A399632         # n has the form p*q^2 with distinct primes iff its exponents are 1 and 2.
%o A399632         if sorted(factorint(n).values()) == [1, 2]:
%o A399632             A.append(n)
%o A399632         t *= r
%o A399632 print(sorted(A))
%Y A399632 Cf. A054753, A246547, A004061, A086122.
%K A399632 nonn,more,new
%O A399632 1,1
%A A399632 _Zephyre Weatherford_, Sep 05 2026
